// @run
// @log 1
// @log 2
// An INLINE-OBJECT union arm carrying a NULLABLE i32-keyed map field. This stopped compiling:
// `emitProgram: i32-keyed map but its map struct was not collected` — a loud emit failure on a
// program that had compiled clean one release earlier.
//
// THE ORPHANED NODE. `parser.vl` mints a real `TypeRef` for each inline-object field, then
// re-encodes a `type X = { … } | …` DECLARATION's right-hand side into a `UnionDecl` from synth
// text WITHOUT re-parsing. The field `TypeRef`s are left behind — reachable in `P.nodes`,
// carrying their spelling, recorded in the arena by no pass. Only the DECLARATION path orphans:
// an inline annotation (`const x: {g: {[i32]: i32}|null} | f64`) does not, nor an `is`-pattern,
// nor a declared struct's members.
//
// WHY THAT BROKE A MINTING FLAG. `nodeTyMapKeyIsI32Span` answers FALSE two different ways —
// "not an i32-keyed map" and "no type recorded for this node" — and only the first is an answer.
// `mI32Used` mints the i32 map struct, the `__map_probe_i32__` helper and a 5-slot scratch
// frame, so under-answering leaves the struct unminted.
//
// NOTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE MAY NAME AN i32-KEYED MAP. `mI32Used` is MODULE-WIDE, so any other
// i32-keyed spelling anywhere here would set the flag and cover the miss — the same warning
// `emit_collect.vl` and `maps/i32-keyed-variant-field.vl` both already carry. A first draft of
// this file kept a non-nullable control beside `Box` and was byte-identical on both builds,
// testing nothing; that control now lives in `union-arm-nonnull-i32-map-field.vl`. The
// string-keyed `Str` below is safe precisely because it is not i32-keyed.
type Box = { g: {[i32]: i32} | null } | f64
type Str = { g: {[string]: i32} | null } | f64

const x: Box = 1.5
if x is f64 { print(1) } else { print(101) }
const s: Str = 3.5
if s is f64 { print(2) } else { print(102) }
